A1. This does not choose to run on rainy days. ~ SALT
A5. There's one of these borne every minute in a cafeteria. ~ TRAYS
A10. There's a catch to this. ~ SNAP
A14. The best part of an opera—only one person sings it. ~ ARIA
A15. A place where you get towels. ~ HOTEL
A16. The thing you don't get in a baked apple. ~ CORE
A17. Fortunes are made here every day. ~ MINT
A18. Next week's lamb chops. ~ EWE
A19. The end of a good pencil. ~ ERASER
A21. Automatic rejectors. ~ EDITORS
A23. The kind of hat the wife wants. ~ DEAREST
A24. Don't be late to meet this. ~ NOTE
A25. What the offspring of a tapir is called. ~ TAPIR
A26. A bar that's worth its weight in gold. ~ INGOT
A28. Eternity to a poet. ~ EER
A29. Just a little jack. ~ LEVER
A33. This is almost entirely surrounded by water and full of liquor (abbr.). ~ NA
A34. The master in the house. ~ SERVANT
A37. It's a gift. ~ DOLE
A38. This is under your very nose. ~ LIP
A40. You'll regret it if you do this. ~ RUE
A41. Where the beer racket started. ~ INN
A43. French name. ~ NOM
A44. Nobody lives for this. ~ EVER
A46. Gave. ~ GRANTED
A49. Here's a pretty scramble for you. ~ PI
A50. Early American skyscraper. ~ TEPEE
A52. This is often in hot water but gets hardened to it. ~ EGG
A53. A waiting man. ~ VALET
A55. To do this your cue must be on the up and up. ~ MASSE
A57. Where this word belongs. ~ HERE
A58. A shallow dive. ~ CABARET
A61. Stakes one's winnings again. ~ PARLAYS
A64. vs. ~ AVERSE
A65. Pop, the Weasel. ~ DAD
A66. "My God!" said the heathen when he saw this. ~ IDOL
A67. Here's a tip for you. ~ PEAK
A68. What grandpa did to grandma down the lane. ~ DROVE
A70. Mischa Elman, roman style. ~ NERO
A71. Fox passes. ~ ERRS
A72. Wise guys. ~ SAGES
A73. What the Seven Little Peppers did and did and did. ~ GREW

D1. Ditto. ~ SAME
D2. What any congressman's speech is. ~ ARID
D3. This comes out in the best of suits. ~ LINING
D4. Women sailors get stuck on. ~ TATTOOS
D5. This word usually has a point to it. ~ THERE
D6. Keeps putting his oar in. ~ ROWS
D7. Wolfed. ~ ATE
D8. An article used by Elizabethans. ~ YE
D9. The rest of the night. ~ SLEEP
D10. Caponied. ~ SCARRED
D11. This has two tunnels at one end and a bridge at the other. ~ NOSE
D12. Old style Jim Londos. ~ ARES
D13. This is fresh. ~ PERT
D20. A hangover on shipboard. ~ RAIL
D22. Dora says this is a kind of nonsense but it seems fishy to us. ~ OTTER
D23. Feminine activity (obsolete). ~ DARNING
D25. This makes the dirt fly. ~ TEA
D26. Something off the main route and all wet. ~ INLET
D27. Related to Dumb Dora. ~ NAIVE
D28. It's useless Tibet you can scale this. ~ EVEREST
D30. This often confronts Hindenburg. ~ VON
D31. What you do when your love runs away with you. ~ ELOPE
D32. A depressing word on pressing bills. ~ REMIT
D35. This is depressed by athlete's foot. ~ RUG
D36. This makes little ones out of big ones. ~ TNT
D39. This makes old men young. ~ PEP
D42. The Raven said this and more. ~ NEVER
D45. The lowdown from the peanut gallery (plur.). ~ REMARKS
D47. The only secret a woman can keep. ~ AGE
D48. What a man calls his wife out loud. ~ DARLING
D51. These are washable and fit under your hat. ~ EARS
D54. The man ahead of you. ~ LEADER
D56. Chicken food (plur.). ~ SEEDS
D57. You'll find a lot of hot women here. ~ HADES
D58. This goes to the opera with M'sieu. ~ CAPE
D59. To swear decently. ~ AVER
D60. No bull about this. ~ BEAR
D61. What they try to do on the level in New York. ~ PAVE
D62. Long, long ago. ~ YORE
D63. Turtleish. ~ SLOW
D65. A seat-grabber. ~ DOG
D69. Egyptian ultra-violet machine. ~ RA


